is why I altered my car to not look like anybody's&nbsp;
car and I will say this whenever I do have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:40.480
luxury of valet parking my car the valet parkers&nbsp;
love it and I always just say get my Batmobile.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.960
There you go. Film Courage: Would you consider&nbsp;
yourself a businessman? Chris: I definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:52.560
would consider myself a business person from&nbsp;
this standpoint the most successful artists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.560 --> 00:03:00.320
that you know about that you have awareness of&nbsp;
whether it be Steven Spielberg as a filmmaker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:07.120
or other filmmakers Steven Soderbergh and&nbsp;
this is the challenge of working in film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:14.960
is that you have to be wildly creative on the&nbsp;
one hand and that describes a lot of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:22.000
but the ones that succeed, the ones that&nbsp;
work consistently have an output of work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:28.400
are the ones that are very good at business and&nbsp;
so that is I think the biggest challenge facing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:34.640
filmmakers is to balance the artist side which&nbsp;
a lot of times conflicts with being a business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:39.680
person because what I learned about being about&nbsp;
being successful in business is it's all about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:48.080
deconstructing and doing kind of a little&nbsp;
autopsy on your process and then coming up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:52.640
with the successful parts of the process&nbsp;
and then repeating it. For example having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:57.120
written a couple of books non-fiction in the&nbsp;
nonfiction realm I would always have friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:02.720
ask well how did you write a book and I said I&nbsp;
didn't write a book I wrote a thousand words a day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:08.480
and I did that every day and I did it five&nbsp;
days a week because I got up at five a.m and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:14.160
I worked till 8 am when I was spent I had about&nbsp;
a thousand words. On a good day 3,000 words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:21.440
on a bad day maybe 500 words but I did that&nbsp;
every day 5 days a week never not for months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:30.480
and within 60-70 days I had enough words for a&nbsp;
book the average book is 60 to 70,000 words so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:38.080
what you don't know is before that I spent six&nbsp;
months on an outline for that book so what I began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:44.560
to learn and just through many sort of business&nbsp;
wonky books that I have read I tend to sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:47.760
glean there's always like a read a book like&nbsp;
the Four Hour Work Week [Author Tim Ferriss] or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:50.720
